   ],
   "text": "Drawn from ElectroSmash's \"Boss DS-1 Distortion Analysis\" (schematic and\nparts list, part numbers as there): the DS-1A circuit, true bypass.\nhttps://electrosmash.com/boss-ds1-analysis"
  }
 ],
 "notes": {
  "part:D1": "Reverse-polarity protection, drawn as a shunt across the supply as on the Boss board (a 1N4001 standing in for the 1N4004).",
  "part:R24": "R24 and R25 halve the supply to make VB, the 4.5 V the whole circuit is biased at.",
  "part:Q1": "Input buffer: an emitter follower biased at VB through R2, so the pickup sees about 470k.",
  "part:Q2": "Booster: a common-emitter stage whose gain is held down by R7 and C4 from collector to base. About 35 dB, so loud notes already clip softly here.",
  "part:R9": "Sets the booster's gain with R8; a larger R9 (1k is a common mod) lowers it a lot and leaves more range to Distortion.",
  "part:D8": "Stops a hard swing from Q2 pulling the op-amp's + input below ground.",
  "part:IC1A": "A buffer between the booster and the gain stage.",
  "part:VR1": "Distortion: gain 1 + (pin 1 to wiper) / (wiper to pin 3 + R13), from 0 dB to about 27 dB.",
  "part:C8": "With R13, the gain stage's bass cut: below about 72 Hz the gain falls, so low notes clip less.",
  "part:C7": "Takes the gain down at the very top of the band, against fizz and oscillation.",
  "part:D4": "D4 and D5 clip the signal to VB at about ±0.7 V: the DS-1's hard clipping.",
  "part:C10": "With R14, a treble cut on the clipped signal (about 7 kHz while the diodes are off).",
  "part:VR3": "Tone: R16 and C12 make a low-pass, C11 and R17 a high-pass, and the pot fades between them, with a mid scoop at noon.",
  "part:VR2": "Level.",
  "part:Q3": "Output buffer, the same emitter follower as the input's."
 },
